// automations.memql
//
// The calls capability's two automations, backed by integrations/calls: the media server's
// webhooks applied as they arrive (events.go), and reconciliation (reconcile.go), which
// repairs whatever a webhook missed or reported out of order. An automation loads only from
// its domain's automations.memql (dsl/construct_placement.go), which is why this file exists.

/// Repair every open call against what its media server shows, every thirty seconds.
///
/// WHY A PASS AT ALL. A media server's own list of who is connected is the truth about a call, and the
/// rows only follow it: a participant who drops without a word leaves a seat holding one of the server's
/// seats; a removed participant can come back on a pass a self-hosted server cannot revoke; a waiter who
/// closed their tab leaves a place in line; and a host who left leaves a countdown that only a later
/// request would otherwise notice. The pass reads each call's participant list and repairs the call's rows
/// to it, call by call, under each call's own locks (callsReconcile). It mints no pass: a waiter whose turn
/// has come is let in on its own next poll.
///
/// PLACED on one bff replica at a time, behind the cluster lease `calls-reconcile`: the pass is the
/// cluster's work, not a replica's, and two replicas running it at once would only repeat each other.
/// Every call is still decided under its own locks, so a lease that changes hands mid-pass costs a repeat
/// and nothing else. single: a pass that outlasts thirty seconds is not overlapped by the next one in this
/// process.
@trigger(schedule="*/30 * * * * *", node="bff", lease="calls-reconcile")
@mode(single)
automation reconcileCalls {
  builtin callsReconcile()
}

/// Apply each of the media server's webhooks to the call it names, as it arrives.
///
/// WHY. A webhook is the media server saying, within a moment, who connected, who disconnected, whose screen
/// share is new and which room it closed: the timely half of keeping a call's rows true. Reconciliation is
/// the other half, every thirty seconds, and the truth when the two disagree, because a webhook can be late,
/// reordered or lost; so each event is applied only when it is newer than what the rows already record, and
/// a disconnect only records the seat's last sign of life, leaving the seat's closing to the pass (a page
/// refresh disconnects exactly as a hang-up does).
///
/// THE ROW, NOT THE EVENT. The receiver at /inbound/livekit verified the delivery with the calls media
/// credential and staged it; this hands the builtin the staged row's id and nothing else, and the builtin
/// reads the source, the body and the verdict from the row itself.
///
/// FILTERED ON `received`, like every automation on a staged delivery (dsl/platform/automations.memql
/// says why): the builtin stamps the row it worked, every write publishes node.created, and a trigger
/// admitting the stamp would fire this again. It runs on the node that staged the delivery, which a
/// delivery never leaves (component/node routing.go), so either bff replica works a webhook it received.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:platform:inboundRequest")
@filter(row => row.source == "livekit" && row.status == "received")
@journalOnWrite
automation applyMediaEvent {
  args {
    id  any
  }
  apply := builtin callsApplyMediaEvent(requestId: args.id)
}
